```js
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
import { route } from './routes.mjs';
import { cache } from './cache.mjs';
import { repository } from './repository.mjs';

const c = cache();
const r = repository();

assert.equal(route('preview', 'A', 'item', c, r).price, 10);
assert.equal(route('purchase', 'A', 'item', c, r).price, 10);
assert.equal(r.reads(), 1); // Same pair across routes must share an entry.

assert.equal(route('purchase', 'B', 'item', c, r).price, 90);
// Current code predicts 10 here: B reuses A's item-only cache entry.
assert.equal(r.reads(), 2);
```

A repair should give both routes one shared domain identity policy that represents the pair without ambiguity for opaque strings, and have the service use the already resolved principal. A nested map keyed first by tenant and then by item is one collision-free representation; a delimiter-concatenated string is not sufficient without an encoding that proves uniqueness.

